    <p>In Table 11.1, we see the relationship between the equation in standard form and the properties of the parabola. The How To box lists the steps for graphing a parabola in the standard form $$x = a\left( {y - k} \right)^{2} + h.$$ We will use this procedure in the next example.</p>
    <example number="11.16">
      <problem>
        <p>Graph $$x = 2\left( {y - 2} \right)^{2} + 1$$ using properties.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Identify the constants a, h, k.</cell>
              <cell>$$a = 2,$$$$h = 1,$$$$k = 2$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since $$a = 2,$$ the parabola opens to the right.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The axis of symmetry is $$y = k.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{135mu}$$The axis of symmetry is $$y = 2.$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The vertex is $$\left( {h,k} \right).$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{135mu}$$The vertex is $$\left( {1,2} \right).$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the x-intercept by substituting $$y = 0.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} x &amp; = &amp; {2\left( {y - 2} \right)^{2} + 1} \\ x &amp; = &amp; {2\left( {0 - 2} \right)^{2} + 1} \\ x &amp; = &amp; 9 \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{135mu}$$The x-intercept is $$\left( {9,0} \right).$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the point symmetric to $$\left( {9,0} \right)$$ across theaxis of symmetry.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{135mu}\left( {9,4} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the y-intercepts. Let $$x = 0.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} x &amp; = &amp; {2\left( {y - 2} \right)^{2} + 1} \\ 0 &amp; = &amp; {2\left( {y - 2} \right)^{2} + 1} \\ -1 &amp; = &amp; {2\left( {y - 2} \right)^{2}} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>A square cannot be negative, so there is no realsolution. So there are no y-intercepts.</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Graph the parabola.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
    </example>
    <note type="Try It" number="11.31">
