    <p>In the next example, we notice the a is negative and so the parabola opens to the left.</p>
    <example number="11.17">
      <problem>
        <p>Graph $$x = -4\left( {y + 1} \right)^{2} + 4$$ using properties.</p>
      </problem>
      <solution>
        <table>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Identify the constants a, h, k.</cell>
              <cell>$$a = -4,$$$$h = 4,$$$$k = -1$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Since $$a = -4,$$ the parabola opens to the left.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell />
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The axis of symmetry is $$y = k.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}$$The axis of symmetry is $$y = -1.$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>The vertex is $$\left( {h,k} \right).$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}$$The vertex is $$\left( {4,-1} \right).$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the x-intercept by substituting $$y = 0.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{31mu}\begin{array}{rll} x &amp; = &amp; {-4\left( {y + 1} \right)^{2} + 4} \\ x &amp; = &amp; {-4\left( {0 + 1} \right)^{2} + 4} \\ x &amp; = &amp; 0 \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}$$The x-intercept is $$\left( {0,0} \right).$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the point symmetric to $$\left( {0,0} \right)$$ across theaxis of symmetry.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu}\left( {0,-2} \right)$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Find the y-intercepts.</cell>
              <cell>$$\mspace{144mu} x = -4\left( {y + 1} \right)^{2} + 4$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Let $$x = 0.$$</cell>
              <cell>$$\begin{array}{rll} 0 &amp; = &amp; {-4\left( {y + 1} \right)^{2} + 4} \\ -4 &amp; = &amp; {-4\left( {y + 1} \right)^{2}} \\ 1 &amp; = &amp; \left( {y + 1} \right)^{2} \\ {y + 1} &amp; = &amp; {\pm 1} \end{array}$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$y = -1 + 1\mspace{27mu} y = -1 - 1$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>$$y = 0\mspace{72mu} y = -2$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell />
              <cell>The y-intercepts are $$\left( {0,0} \right)$$ and $$\left( {0,-2} \right).$$</cell>
            </row>
            <row>
              <cell>Graph the parabola.</cell>
              <cell />
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </solution>
